---
import SoftwareLayout from '../../../layouts/SoftwareLayout.astro';
import data from '../../../../public/demos/aero/driver-seegmiller-2026-11-22/comparison.json';
import followup from '../../../../public/demos/aero/driver-seegmiller-2026-11-22/cf-bias-followup.json';
import metadata from '../../../../public/demos/aero/driver-seegmiller-2026-11-22/benchmark-metadata.json';
const base = '/demos/aero/driver-seegmiller-2026-11-22/';
const fine = data.runs.fine;
const signed = (v: number, places = 4) => `${v >= 0 ? '+' : ''}${v.toFixed(places)}`;
const meshes = Object.entries(data.runs);
const examples = followup.points.filter(p => [1.804, 20.196, 35.994].includes(p.x_H));
---

<SoftwareLayout title="Aero CFD vs experiment · Driver–Seegmiller benchmark"
  description="Three real OpenFOAM meshes compared with peer-reviewed experimental data. Inspect the measured deltas, the skin-friction misses, AI attribution and reproducible solver evidence."
  canonicalPath="/software/aero/driver-seegmiller-benchmark/" hideHeader>
  <article class="benchmark">
    <nav class="bench-nav" aria-label="Benchmark navigation"><a href="/software/aero/">Aero overview</a><a href="/software/aero/cfd-solvers/">CFD solver guide</a><a href="#evidence">Data and reproducibility</a></nav>
    <header class="hero">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">EXPERIMENTAL CFD COMPARISON · RUN 22 NOV 2026</p>
        <h1>A real experiment.<br/>A result we can question.</h1>
        <p class="lede">Aero’s OpenFOAM pipeline meets the Driver–Seegmiller backward-facing step: three meshes, measured velocity profiles, wall pressure and skin friction.</p>
        <p class="attribution">AI workflow assistance: <strong>{metadata.ai_workflow_model}</strong> <span>(owner-reported model label)</span></p>
        <div class="actions"><a class="primary" href="#results">Inspect the deltas</a><a href={base+'reproducibility.zip'} download>Download cases and evidence</a></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="headline-result" aria-label="Finest-mesh reattachment result"><p>REATTACHMENT DELTA</p><strong>{signed(fine.reattachment_delta_percent, 2)}<span>%</span></strong><p>{fine.reattachment_x_H.toFixed(4)}H predicted<br/>6.26 ± 0.10H measured</p><div class="result-status">Comparison complete<br/><b>Validation not established</b></div></aside>
    </header>
    <div class="disposition"><strong>Numerical qualification remains open.</strong> Refined runs missed the 10⁻⁶ initial-residual target, and mesh independence is unproven. The finest result lies outside the reported reattachment interval. These are provisional comparison results.</div>
    <p class="publication">Published <time datetime="2026-11-23">23 November 2026</time> by Alex Blythe. The reference experiment is peer-reviewed; this Aero execution report is not. This is a retained OpenFOAM run, separate from Aero’s Excel and browser demonstrations.</p>

    <section id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">MEASURED DIFFERENCES</p><h2 id="results-title">The finest mesh, against the experiment.</h2>
      <div class="metrics">
        <div><span>Streamwise velocity RMSE</span><strong>{(fine.u_over_Uref_all_stations.rmse*100).toFixed(2)}%</strong><small>of Uref · {fine.u_dimensional_rmse_m_s.toFixed(2)} m/s · 129 locations</small></div>
        <div><span>Pressure coefficient RMSE</span><strong>{fine.Cp.rmse.toFixed(5)}</strong><small>33 lower-wall measurements</small></div>
        <div><span>Skin-friction coefficient RMSE</span><strong>{fine.Cf.rmse.toFixed(7)}</strong><small>20 lower-wall measurements</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Same inputs and schemes; independently generated nested orthogonal meshes.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Mesh / cells</th><th scope="col">Reattachment x/H</th><th scope="col">CFD − experiment</th><th scope="col">Velocity RMSE / Uref</th><th scope="col">Initial-residual target</th></tr></thead><tbody>{meshes.map(([name, r]) => <tr><th scope="row">{name} / {r.cells.toLocaleString('en-US')}</th><td>{r.reattachment_x_H.toFixed(4)}</td><td>{signed(r.reattachment_delta_H)}H · {signed(r.reattachment_delta_percent, 2)}%</td><td>{(r.u_over_Uref_all_stations.rmse*100).toFixed(2)}%</td><td>{r.all_last_initial_residuals_below_1e_6 ? 'Met' : 'Not met'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <p>Fine-grid reattachment is {fine.reattachment_delta_mm.toFixed(2)} mm longer than measured. Medium-to-fine reattachment changes by {data.mesh_sensitivity.reattachment_medium_to_fine_percent_of_fine.toFixed(2)}% of the fine result. The grid increments do not establish an asymptotic trend or a defensible extrapolated uncertainty bound.</p>
      <figure><img src={base+'velocity_comparison.png'} width="2520" height="900" alt="Measured and calculated velocity profiles on three meshes at five streamwise positions"/><figcaption>The plot enlarges the lower-wall region. Statistics use all published points; downstream-only velocity RMSE is {(fine.u_over_Uref_downstream_only.rmse*100).toFixed(2)}% of Uref across 112 measurements.</figcaption></figure>
    </section>

    <section id="skin-friction" aria-labelledby="skin-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE FAILURE PATTERN</p><h2 id="skin-title">Why did only 5 of 20 friction points agree?</h2>
      <p class="lede">Every upstream point falls inside its reported error bar. Downstream, only 2 of 17 do—and all 17 predictions fall below the measured coefficient.</p>
      <figure><img src={base+'wall_comparison.png'} width="2160" height="864" loading="lazy" alt="Experimental wall pressure and skin friction compared with coarse, medium and fine CFD predictions; most downstream friction predictions are low"/><figcaption>Bars show the experimental Cf file’s listed errors. Their confidence level is unspecified. Agreement means |Cf CFD − Cf experiment| ≤ listed error; no missing experimental uncertainty was invented.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Selected fine-grid misses. Coefficients are dimensionless.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">x/H</th><th scope="col">Measured Cf</th><th scope="col">Aero Cf</th><th scope="col">Reported error ±</th><th scope="col">NASA CFL3D SSTm Cf</th></tr></thead><tbody>{examples.map(p => <tr><th scope="row">{p.x_H.toFixed(3)}</th><td>{p.experiment.toFixed(6)}</td><td>{p.our_Cf.toFixed(6)}</td><td>{p.experimental_error.toFixed(6)}</td><td>{p.NASA_SST_Cf.toFixed(6)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <div class="findings">
        <div><h3>Persistent downstream bias</h3><p>The calculation gives more-negative wall shear in the separation region and too little positive friction after reattachment. Near x/H = 36, friction is about 21% low against a listed experimental error of ±8%.</p></div>
        <div><h3>A model contribution is plausible</h3><p>Interpolating NASA’s independent CFL3D SSTm reference onto these same 20 measurements gives {followup.NASA_related_SST_reference_pass_count}/20 inside the listed errors. It shows a related bias. That reference uses another implementation and a low-Mach, quasi-steady compressible setup; it is supporting evidence, not an identical controlled test of our OpenFOAM model.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Numerical error is still present</h3><p>Mesh and near-wall treatment have not been isolated. At the measured locations, the largest final three-snapshot Cf range is {fine.Cf.max_tail_range_at_experimental_positions.toExponential(3)}, much smaller than several observed misses. This supports persistent bias, while the unmet residual target prevents a complete numerical qualification.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p>Controlled mesh, near-wall and inflow comparisons are needed to separate these effects. This benchmark does not establish that SST alone caused all the error.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="method" aria-labelledby="method-title"><p class="eyebrow">REPRODUCIBLE SETUP</p><h2 id="method-title">What actually ran.</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table><tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Reference</th><td>Driver &amp; Seegmiller (1985), AIAA Journal 23(2), 163–171; zero upper-wall divergence case; NASA-curated experimental data.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Operating point</th><td>H = 0.0127 m; Uref = 44.2 m/s; corrected ReH = 36,000; ν = 1.55927778 × 10⁻⁵ m²/s.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Domain</th><td>x/H = −130…50; upstream height 8H, downstream height 9H; viscous walls begin at −110H; one empty spanwise cell.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Execution</th><td>OpenFOAM Foundation 10, build 10-c4cf895ad8fa; CPU; simpleFoam; kOmegaSST; incompressible steady RANS.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Wall treatment</th><td>Spalding nut wall function and omegaWallFunction. Fine lower-wall sampled y+ mean {fine.lower_wall_yplus.mean.toFixed(2)}, maximum {fine.lower_wall_yplus.max.toFixed(2)}; wall-resolution suitability is not automatically established by these values.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Inlet turbulence</th><td>0.061% intensity; νt/ν = 0.009, following NASA’s SST comparison convention.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Numerics</th><td>Limited linear gradients; linearUpwind velocity; upwind k and ω; fixed schemes and relaxation across the three meshes.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Iterations</th><td>Coarse 1,500; medium 1,800; fine 2,000 followed by a 1,500-iteration continuation, for 3,500 total. SIMPLE iterations are not physical seconds.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p>The inlet amplitude was conditioned once using an upstream-only run, then fixed at 41.62491363497 m/s for all meshes. No downstream measurement was fitted. The fine run’s actual upstream reference speed is {fine.reference_speed_actual_m_s.toFixed(5)} m/s, {(fine.reference_speed_delta_percent).toFixed(3)}% from the target. Remaining profile-shape differences contribute uncertainty.</p>
      <p>All differences are CFD minus experiment. Velocity normalization uses the fixed measured Uref, not a per-mesh fitted denominator. Cp = 2(p − p at 40H)/Uref² with kinematic pressure; Cf = −2τx/Uref² using OpenFOAM’s inward-normal wall-stress convention. Linear interpolation maps CFD samples onto measurement coordinates. Reattachment is the Cf zero crossing after the main reverse-flow minimum.</p>
      <p>RMSE is unweighted over published points. The report compares mean u/v, lower-wall Cp/Cf and reattachment; it does not compare the published Reynolds stresses or triple moments. Compressibility, three-dimensional effects and full experimental uncertainty are not separately quantified. The original article’s full text was unavailable from NTRS; the primary metadata, NASA case instructions and numerical tables were reviewed.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="convergence-title"><p class="eyebrow">SETTLING AND OPEN GATES</p><h2 id="convergence-title">A completed run is a separate claim from validation.</h2>
      <figure><img src={base+'fine_convergence.png'} width="1620" height="720" loading="lazy" alt="Fine-grid reattachment history approaching a plateau above the experimental interval, with the checkpoint continuation marked"/><figcaption>Over the final three snapshots, reattachment ranges by {fine.tail_reattachment_range_H.toFixed(6)}H. The continuation retained the same physics and numerical settings.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>The initial fine warm start transferred only wall diagnostics because the medium run’s latest retained time had no complete U/p fields. Its primary fields therefore started uniformly. The continuation used the complete fine-grid checkpoint at iteration 2,000. A guard now requires complete solved fields for future mapped starts. The medium comparison retains valid sampled data, although its final complete volume fields were not collected.</p>
      <p>A necessary imported-case fix preserved the supplied polyMesh and still ran checkMesh. All three meshes passed checkMesh; maximum non-orthogonality was zero. All 37 existing pipeline tests passed, and an independent arithmetic audit recomputed the pointwise differences, RMSEs and raw-stress reattachment root. Those checks establish execution and arithmetic evidence, not physical accuracy.</p>
      <p>Fine final initial residuals include p = {fine.last_initial_residuals.p.toExponential(3)} and k = {fine.last_initial_residuals.k.toExponential(3)}, above the selected 10⁻⁶ criterion. Fine inlet/outlet flux imbalance is {fine.mass_imbalance_fraction.toExponential(3)} as a fraction of inlet flow. Small imbalance does not establish solution accuracy.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="attribution" aria-labelledby="attribution-title"><p class="eyebrow">AI AND NUMERICAL PROVENANCE</p><h2 id="attribution-title">OpenAI 6.1 High assisted the workflow.</h2>
      <p><strong>Model attribution: OpenAI 6.1 High</strong>, as supplied by project owner Alex Blythe. The retained runtime artifacts do not independently capture the model identity.</p>
      <p>The model assisted case preparation, Aero pipeline orchestration, postprocessing, diagnosis and documentation. <strong>OpenFOAM produced the numerical CFD predictions.</strong> This is one AI-assisted engineering workflow execution, not a controlled comparison of language models or evidence that an LLM itself solved the flow equations.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-title"><p class="eyebrow">OPEN EVIDENCE</p><h2 id="evidence-title">Read the values. Replay the cases.</h2>
      <div class="downloads">
        <a class="primary" href={base+'reproducibility.zip'} download>Complete public reproducibility bundle</a>
        <a href={base+'pointwise_deltas.csv'} download>933 pointwise deltas · CSV</a>
        <a href={base+'comparison.json'}>Full comparison · JSON</a>
        <a href={base+'cf-bias-followup.json'}>Skin-friction follow-up</a>
        <a href={base+'residual_history.csv'} download>Equation residual histories</a>
        <a href={base+'fine_convergence.csv'} download>Fine-grid convergence samples</a>
        <a href={base+'arithmetic_audit.json'}>Arithmetic audit</a>
        <a href={base+'benchmark-metadata.json'}>Model attribution and scope</a>
        <a href={base+'manifest.json'}>Public asset SHA-256 manifest</a>
      </div>
      <p>The bundle contains retained case inputs, mesh files, solver logs, sampled results, complete retained fine-grid fields, public reference data and comparison scripts. Run checkMesh and simpleFoam on a writable copy of each case_input. The fine checkpoint is already mapped into its starting fields. The README records the 2,000 + 1,500 continuation and the medium-field retention limitation.</p>
      <p>The package is restricted to this public experiment. Private Aero toolkit modules, credentials, employer information and live-service configuration are excluded. Hashes establish artifact identity, not scientific correctness.</p>
      <h3>Primary sources</h3><ul>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.2514/3.8890">Driver &amp; Seegmiller, “Features of a Reattaching Turbulent Shear Layer in Divergent Channel Flow,” AIAA Journal (1985)</a>; <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19850039701">NASA publication record</a>.</li>
        <li><a href="https://tmbwg.github.io/turbmodels/backstep_val.html">NASA experimental case and measurements</a>; <a href="https://tmbwg.github.io/turbmodels/backstep_grids.html">boundary geometry and grids</a>.</li>
        <li><a href="https://tmbwg.github.io/turbmodels/backstep_val_sst.html">NASA SSTm reference and steady-convergence qualification</a>; <a href="https://www.grc.nasa.gov/WWW/wind/valid/backstep/backstep01/backstep01.html">NASA dimensional operating point</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <footer>Public experiment comparison · Aero · Alex Blythe · Published 23 November 2026</footer>
  </article>
</SoftwareLayout>

